  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will remain in firm control overall through\nSaturday, leading to ongoing dry weather and above-normal\ntemperatures. A couple of passing disturbances through then will\ndo little to change this.\n\nA crossing 500mb shortwave tonight into Wednesday morning will\nprovide some clouds, but moisture levels, particularly in the\nsub-cloud layer, remain too low to support any precipitation. A\ncrossing frontal boundary Thursday night/Friday morning is\nlikewise currently expected to be dry. However, model blends\ncontinue to show a very small (less than 15 percent) chance for\nmeasurable rain north of I-80, which could be realized if enough\nLake Erie moisture becomes involved. \n\nRain chances finally return to the forecast in earnest starting\nSunday, as a Southern Plains disturbance tracks eastward towards\nthe Ohio Valley. The chances of significant rainfall remain low,\nas chances of a half-inch of rain or more over any 48-hour\nperiod into early next week remain below 50 percent. However,\nensembles suggest that a stronger closed 500mb low could develop\nover the central Plains by 12Z Tuesday. Depending on if/how this\nlow develops and evolves, a wetter and more active pattern is a\npossibility at some point next week.",
